In some references, Kummer's three conditions for FLT to hold with prime exponent p are:
1. The class number of the p-th cyclotomic field is not divisible by p
2. p^2 does not divide the numerator of any Bernoulli number B_2, B_4, ..., B_{p-3}
3. p does not divide the numerator of any Bernoulli number B_2, B_4, ..., B_{p-3}

Hmm, let me reconsider what "three conditions" means. 

Actually, I think the "three conditions" might refer to the three conditions that Kummer needed to be satisfied for his proof to work:
1. The ring of cyclotomic integers Z[ζ_p] has unique factorization (or at least, the class number is not divisible by p)
2. p^2 does not divide any of the Bernoulli numbers B_2, B_4, ..., B_{p-3}
3. p does not divide any of the Bernoulli numbers B_2, B_4, ..., B_{p-3}

I'm going to go with the interpretation that the "three conditions" are the three levels of regularity:
1. Regular: p doesn't divide the class number (equivalently, p doesn't divide any B_{2k} for 0 < 2k < p-1)
2. Extra-regular: p² doesn't divide any B_{2k}
3. Triple-regular (or some higher order): p³ doesn't divide any B_{2k}
